<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <POU Name="F_AttenuatorOK" Id="{860b1a67-b309-4c7c-986b-4453f9f031c8}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
F_AttenuatorOK : is the attenuator safe for a device, given its status word?

Purpose:
    Decide whether a device under PMPS protection may treat the attenuator as
    safe while it is in the reported state. This reduces the raw attenuator
    status word to the single question the device needs answered, and it fails
    closed: any state it cannot prove safe for the device is reported as FALSE.

Usage:
    Evaluate every scan in which the device's beam parameter request is
    asserted, passing the attenuator status word in xStatus (per-bit contract
    at that declaration, matching ST_AttenuatorStatus):

        xAttenuatorOK := F_AttenuatorOK( xStatus := wAttenuatorStatus );

    The result is advisory to the caller's beam parameter logic: this helper
    holds no state, uses no timers and raises no fault of its own - the caller
    decides what to do when the attenuator cannot be trusted.

    In local mode, the attenuator ignores PMPS requests, following a local
    setpoint. In this mode, a device cannot trust that the attenuator will not
    increase transmission beyond its own setpoint. Therefore any indication of
    a changing transmission should cause a fault.

    In PMPS mode, if a device has submitted a preemptive request and received
    acknowledgement, then the transmission setpoint will be governed at least
    by that device's setpoint. Therefore, the attenuator may be moving without
    generating a fault.

Objectives:
    - One place that encodes the "can the attenuator be trusted right now"
      decision, instead of each device open-coding the status word bits.
    - Fail closed: an uninterpretable status word, and any status word whose
      OK bit is clear, return FALSE.
    - Spell out the status word contract here so a caller can tell which bit
      a given state is reported on, and so a change to the layout has one
      documented consumer to update.

Decision table (bit numbers are status word bit indices):
    LocalMode (bit 2) set      : TRUE only when AtTarget (bit 0) is set and
                                 Moving (bit 1) is clear and OK (bit 4) is
                                 set. Under a local setpoint the device
                                 cannot bound transmission, so a local
                                 attenuator that is not at target is not
                                 trusted, and motion under a local setpoint
                                 always faults.
    else PMPSMode (bit 3) set  : TRUE, provided OK (bit 4) is set. Motion is
                                 expected, because transmission is bounded by
                                 the acknowledged device setpoint.
    neither mode bit set       : FALSE - the status word is not
                                 interpretable, so it is not trusted.
    both mode bits set         : treated as local mode; the LocalMode branch
                                 is tested first and wins.

History:
    2020-11-05  Alex Wallace : Created - a BP compare for transmission.
    2026-11-27  Scirix Agent : Header given purpose, usage, objectives and the
                               status word decision table, plus the history
                               and the xStatus bit layout comment. Comments
                               only - no code change (audit finding #268).
*)
FUNCTION F_AttenuatorOK : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
    // Attenuator status word: one bit per status flag, no units. The bit
    // order is that of ST_AttenuatorStatus (DUTs/InternalDUT):
    //   bit 0 AtTarget  : TRUE when the attenuator has reached its commanded
    //                     target transmission.
    //   bit 1 Moving    : TRUE while the blades are moving, i.e. while the
    //                     transmission is changing.
    //   bit 2 LocalMode : TRUE when the attenuator ignores PMPS requests and
    //                     follows its own local setpoint.
    //   bit 3 PMPSMode  : TRUE when the attenuator takes its setpoints from
    //                     PMPS only.
    //   bit 4 OK        : TRUE when the attenuator reports itself healthy.
    //                     When this bit is clear the function always returns
    //                     FALSE.
    //   bit 5 Toggle    : Heartbeat/toggle bit - not consulted here.
    //   bit 6 Include   : Device-inclusion flag - not consulted here.
    // Bits 7-15 are not consulted.
    xStatus : WORD;
END_VAR
VAR
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[IF xStatus.2 THEN // Local mode
    F_AttenuatorOK := xStatus.0 and NOT xStatus.1;
ELSIF xStatus.3 AND NOT xStatus.2 THEN // PMPS mode
    F_AttenuatorOK := TRUE;
END_IF

F_AttenuatorOK := xStatus.4 AND F_AttenuatorOK;]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>